Chemistry/General-Chemistry/06-Gases/Gas.content:
Definition
The gas constant R is a constant in the Ideal Gas Equation. Using the SI Units gives:
R = \frac{PV}{nT} = \frac{101,325 Pa \times 22.4140 \times 10^{-2} m^3}{1 mol \times 273.15K} = 8.3145 \frac{Pa \, m^3}{mol \, K}
However, the pascal units are \frac{kg}{ms^2}, so the units of m^3Pa become \frac{kgm^2}{s^2}, which is the SI unit of Energy.
R = 8.3145 \frac{J}{molK}

Chemistry/General-Chemistry/14-Solutions.content:
Definition
A concentration unit that is independent of Temperature but also proportional to Mole fraction in Dilute Solutions is molality (m), the number of moles of Solute per kilogram of Solvent (not of Solution). A solution in which 1.00 moles of urea, \ce{CO(NH2)},  is dissolved in 1.00 kg of Water is described as a 1.00 molal solution and designated as 1.00 m \ce{CO(NH2)2}. Molality is defines as
\text{molality}(m) = \frac{\text{amount of solute (in moles)}}{\text{amount of solvent (in kilograms)}}

Chemistry/General-Chemistry/16-Acids-and.content:
Definition
The acid ionization constant, K_a , is a quantitative measure of an Acid strength in Solution, representing the Equilibrium Constant for its Dissociation into Ions. A higher K_a indicates greater Ionization and a Stronger Acid.
K_a = \frac{[\ce{H+}] [\ce{A-}]}{[\ce{HA}]}

Definition
The degree of ionization, \alpha, is the proportion of neutral Molecules that Dissociate into Ions in a Solution or Gas, calculated as the ratio of Ionized molecules to the total molecules initially taken. It indicates the strength of Electrolytes, with Strong Acids and Strong Bases having a high \alpha and Weak Acids and Weak Bases having a low \alpha.
\alpha = \frac{\text{Number of ionized molecules}}{\text{Total number of molecules taken}}

Chemistry/General-Chemistry/17-Acid-Base.content:
Definition
The Henderson Hasselbalch equation is used to describe a Buffer Solution, by deriving an Acid Ionization Constant. Consider a Mixture of a hypothetical Weak Acid, \ce{HA} and its Salt, \ce{NaA}.
\ce{HA} + \ce{H2O} \ce{\leftrightharpoons} \ce{H3O+} + \ce{A-}
The acid ionization constant is
K_a = \frac{[\ce{H3O+}] [\ce{A-}]}{[\ce{HA}]}
Next, we take the negative log of each side of this Chemical Equation.
-\log K_a = -\log[\ce{H3O+}] - \log \frac{[\ce{A-}]}{[\ce{HA}]}
Now, recall pH and pKa formulas.
pK_A = pH - \log \frac{[\ce{A-}]}{[\ce{HA}]}
Solve for pH
pH = pK_a + \log \frac{[\ce{A-}]}{[\ce{HA}]}
\ce{A-} is the Conjugate Base of the weak acid \ce{HA}.
pH = pK_a + \log \frac{\text{[conjugate base]}}{\text{[acid]}}

Chemistry/General-Chemistry/19-Electroch.content:
