Math/Linear-Algebra/05-Vector-Spaces/Nul.content:
Definition
The null space is a Subspace consisting of all input Vectors \mathbf{x} that are mapped to the Zero Vector when multiplied by a Matrix \mathbf{A}.
\mathbf{Ax=0}

Math/Linear-Algebra/05-Vector-Spaces/Vec.content:
Overview
A vector space is a Set of Vectors or Matrix where the operation of Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication adhere to 8 axioms:

Associativity Of Addition for vectors: \mathbf{u+(v+w)=(u+v)+w}
Commutativity Of Addition for vectors: \mathbf{u+v=v+u}
Additive Identity for vectors: There Exists an Element Of the set \mathbf{0} \in V called the Zero Vector, such that \mathbf{v+0=v}
Additive Inverse for vectors: There exists an element of the set -\mathbf{v} \in V, such that \mathbf{v+(-v)=0}
Compatibility of Scalar Multiplication with Field Multiplication: a(b\mathbf{v})=(ab)\mathbf{v}
Multiplicative Identity for vectors: 1\mathbf{v=v}
Distributivity of scalar multiplication with respect to vector addition: a\mathbf{(v+u)}=a\mathbf{v}+a\mathbf{u}
Distributivity of scalar multiplication with respect to Field addition: (a+b)\mathbf{v}=a\mathbf{v}+b\mathbf{v}

Math/Linear-Algebra/07-Eigenvalues-and-E.content:
Definition
The Eigenvalue equation describes how a Linear Transformation \mathbf{A} acts on a Eigenvector \mathbf{v}, only by scaling it with an Eigenvalue \lambda, without changing the direction.
\mathbf{Av}= \lambda \mathbf{v}

Definition
A trace tr(\mathbf{A}) of a square Matrix \mathbf{A} is the sum of the elements on its main diagonal.
tr(\mathbf{A})=a_{11}+a_{22}+...+a_{nn}

Math/Linear-Algebra/08-Diagonalization/0.content:
Overview
Diagonalization is the process of using a Linear Transformation on a Matrix \mathbf{A} into a Diagonal Matrix by the form \Lambda, by relating it to a Basis composed of its Eigenvectors.
\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{X} \Lambda \mathbf{X}^{-1}

Math/Linear-Algebra/08-Diagonalization/M.content:
Definition
08 Diagonalization simplifies the calculation of Matrix powers. The Diagonal Matrix is simple to multiply.
\mathbf{A}^k = \mathbf{X} \Lambda^k \mathbf{X}^{-1}
